.TH AGNSH 1 "November 2026" "agnoshi 2.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agnsh \- AI-native natural language shell for AGNOS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agnsh
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ -c
.IR COMMAND ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agnsh
is a shell that also understands natural language. A line whose first word
is a program runs as that program; anything else is read as natural
language, classified, reported, and \(em if its translation is SAFE or
READ_ONLY \(em run.
.PP
Without arguments, starts an interactive shell. The prompt carries the
current mode (e.g.
.BR [ASSIST]\ > ).
A natural-language line prints its report \(em the translated command and
its risk level
.RB ( [LOW] " / " [MED] " / " [HIGH] " / " [CRIT] ),
with a
.B Hint:
line when the translation is not a runnable command \(em and then runs it
if it may.
.PP
On a Linux host, an interactive shell started with both standard input and
standard output on
.I /dev/null
\(em as kybernet starts a service \(em opens
.I /dev/console
and runs its session there, on standard input, output and error. It does
not take the console as its controlling terminal. If the console cannot be
opened, it says why on standard error and exits 1. Any other standard
input, including
.I /dev/null
alone, is read as before.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -c, --command \fICMD\fR
Run one line and exit with its status. stdout is the program's output;
agnsh's own notices and prompts go to stderr. For a natural-language line
the report is written to the report folder (see
.BR FILES )
instead of stdout.
.TP
.B -n, --dry-run
With
.BR -c :
run nothing \(em no program, no pipeline, no power verb \(em and print the
report on stdout, as
.B -c
did before 2.0.0.
.TP
.B --mode \fINAME\fR
With
.BR -c ,
run under mode \fINAME\fR (auto, assist, human, strict; default auto). An
unrecognised name is an error and agnsh exits non-zero \(em it is never
silently downgraded to a more permissive mode.
.TP
.B -v, --version
Show version and exit.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show usage and exit.
.SH WHAT RUNS
.TP
.B Shell lines
A bareword program found on
.B $PATH
(only its absolute entries are searched), or
.BR "run /abs/path " [ args ].
On AGNOS also
.BI /bin/ name\fR,
two-stage pipelines
.RB ( "cmd1 | cmd2" ),
output redirection
.RB ( "cmd > file" )
and background jobs
.RB ( "prog &" );
on a Linux host those three are refused, never read as natural language.
Programs run from an argument vector \(em never through a shell \(em and
inherit agnsh's environment.
.TP
.B Natural language
A SAFE or READ_ONLY translation runs. A USER_WRITE, SYSTEM_WRITE or ADMIN
translation asks
.B "approve: ... ? [y/N]"
on standard error, in every mode, and runs only on y. A remove or move is
checkpointed first, and one that cannot be checkpointed is refused; on a
Linux host an ADMIN line runs as
.BR "sudo -n" .
With no answer (end of input, or 300 seconds on a Linux host) nothing runs.
BLOCKED translations, a recursive remove among them, never run.
.TP
.B Restricted sessions
On a Linux host, agnsh running as root (uid or euid 0) is restricted: it
reports every natural-language line and runs none of them, says so before
the first interactive prompt, and exits 126 for such a line under
.BR -c .
Shell lines still run. AGNOS is never restricted.
.TP
.B BLOCKED shell lines
A typed line the classifier calls BLOCKED (rm -rf, dd, mkfs, chmod, chown,
shred) asks for confirmation in every mode; with no answer it is declined.
.SH MODES
Every mode classifies, reports risk and writes an audit record. What the
mode changes is whether a launch is confirmed first. A natural-language line
that needs approval asks in every mode, once: its approval prompt stands in
for the mode's confirmation.
.TP
.B human, strict
Every launch asks for a y/n confirmation on stderr.
.TP
.B assist
Default for the interactive shell. Launches proceed without a prompt.
.TP
.B auto
Default for
.BR -c .
Launches proceed without a prompt.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B the program's status
When it ran (128 +
.I N
when signal
.I N
killed it \(em on AGNOS too, since 2.0.1).
.TP
.B 126
Understood but not run: BLOCKED, declined or not answered at a prompt,
a file that could not be checkpointed, a sudo that could not be used,
arguments refused by the safety checks, or a restricted session (root on
a Linux host).
.TP
.B 127
Nothing to run: not understood, a question, no such program, or it could
not be launched.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error; or, for the interactive shell, no usable input: a read error,
or standard input and output on
.I /dev/null
with no console to open.
.PP
Under
.B --dry-run
the status is 0 for a line that would run.
.SH BUILTINS
.TP
.B help
Show built-in commands.
.TP
.B clear
Clear screen.
.TP
.B exit, quit
Exit shell.
.TP
.B history
Show command history.
.TP
.B cd [\fIDIR\fR|-|~]
Change the working directory: to \fIDIR\fR, to
.B $HOME
with no argument or ~, or back to the previous one with -, which is
printed. On AGNOS, where there is no kernel working directory, agnsh keeps
its own: it resolves its own relative paths against it (a > target, a
natural-language path) and passes it to programs as
.BR PWD .
Exits 1 when the directory cannot be entered.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the working directory.
.TP
.B undo
Put back what the newest approved remove or move changed, from its
checkpoint: each removed file recreated, each move renamed back. It never
overwrites; an entry whose path is taken stays, and the status is 1. Exits 0
when everything was put back, 1 when there was nothing to undo or an entry
stayed.
.TP
.B mode [NAME]
Show or change operating mode (human, assist, auto, strict).
.TP
.B run \fIPATH\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
Run a program from an absolute path.
.TP
.B reboot, poweroff, halt
Platform power control (AGNOS).
.SH PERMISSIONS
Every natural-language translation is classified into one of six levels,
and every typed shell line is checked for BLOCKED:
.TP
.B SAFE
No state change (echo, help).
.TP
.B READ_ONLY
System queries (ls, cat, ps).
.TP
.B USER_WRITE
Modifies user files (cp, mv, touch).
.TP
.B SYSTEM_WRITE
Modifies system paths (requires approval).
.TP
.B ADMIN
Requires sudo (requires approval).
.TP
.B BLOCKED
Never run for the AI (rm -rf, dd, mkfs, chmod, chown, shred); a typed one
runs only after an explicit yes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.agnsh_history
Command history (mode 0600).
.TP
.I ~/.agnsh_audit.log
Structured JSON audit log, mode 0600, opened O_NOFOLLOW. Each line carries
timestamp, user, mode, input, action, approved (0/1), result, and
exit_code (JSON null when the record describes something that never ran).
.I result
comes from one of two disjoint sets \(em parse-time (proposed,
needs_approval, blocked, needs_llm, needs_exec, rejected_safety) or
exec-time (launched, executed, failed, error, denied).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/agnoshi/reports/
The report folder (default
.IR ~/.local/state/agnoshi/reports/ ;
.I /.agnsh_reports/
on AGNOS). One report per natural-language line run with
.BR -c ,
named
.IR YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ - pid .txt ,
a copy in
.IR latest.txt ,
the newest 100 kept. The folder is 0700 and each report 0600; on a Linux
host agnsh refuses a folder that is a symlink, belongs to another user or
is writable by others.
.PP
When
.B $HOME
is unset, the audit log and history fall back to
.IR /tmp/agnsh_audit.log. "" uid
and
.IR /tmp/agnsh_history. "" uid ,
and the report folder to
.IR /tmp/agnoshi-reports. "" uid
\(em degraded operation, not a supported configuration.
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the AGNOS project.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
